{"name":"Olive Rush","hex":"#a39467","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"olive","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A tree of species Olea europaea cultivated since ancient times in the Mediterranean for its fruit and the oil obtained from it.","The small oval fruit of this tree, eaten ripe (usually black) or unripe (usually green)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a grayish green color, that of an unripe olive."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/olive"},{"term":"rush","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A sudden forward motion.","A surge, a sudden increase."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To hurry; to perform a task with great haste, often not properly or without thinking carefully.","To flow or move forward rapidly or noisily."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rush"}],"entries":[{"title":"Olive Rush","blocks":[{"text":"Olive Rush was a painter, illustrator, muralist, and an important pioneer in Native American art education. Her paintings are held in a number of private collections and museums, including: the Brooklyn Museum of New York City, the Haan Mansion Museum of Indiana Art, the Indianapolis Museum of Art, Indiana and the Smithsonian American Art Museum."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Olive_Rush.jpg/330px-Olive_Rush.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olive_Rush","wikidata":"Q2019962"},{"title":"Olive","blocks":[{"text":"The cultivated olive is a subspecies of subtropical evergreen tree in the family Oleaceae. Modern cultivars are traced primarily to the Near East, Aegean Sea, and Strait of Gibraltar. Olea europaea is the type species for its genus, Olea, which lends its name to the Oleaceae plant family. The olive fruit is classed botanically as a drupe, similar in structure and function to the cherry or peach. The term oil was originally synonymous with olive oil."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Olivesfromjordan.jpg/330px-Olivesfromjordan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olive","wikidata":"Q1621080"},{"title":"Olive (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Olive, also called olive green, is a dark shade of yellowish green, like that of unripe or green olives."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Olives_stuffed_with_almonds.jpg/330px-Olives_stuffed_with_almonds.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olive_(color)","wikidata":"Q864152"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Olive-Rush-Mural-NMSU-Biology-Bldg.jpg/250px-Olive-Rush-Mural-NMSU-Biology-Bldg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/54/Olive-Rush-Mural-NMSU-Biology-Bldg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Olive-Rush-Mural-NMSU-Biology-Bldg.jpg/250px-Olive-Rush-Mural-NMSU-Biology-Bldg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A portion of Olive Rush's May 1936 WPA mural at NMSU","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOlive-Rush-Mural-NMSU-Biology-Bldg.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/OliveRush1912.png/330px-OliveRush1912.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/OliveRush1912.png/330px-OliveRush1912.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Olive Rush, from a 1912 publication","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOliveRush1912.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0